Subject: creating data objects using loop index
Message-ID: <000f01c596a6$38c77360$64fea8c0@Ford>

Hi, 

  I am trying to create matrices on the fly and
call them later in order to save some time.  I realize that for loops in S
aren't that efficient, but humor me: 

for(i in 1:10) { 

     object[i]<-A%*%B 

} 

Clearly this isn't creating a new
matrix each time 

I'd like to have matrix1, matrix2, matrix3...etc created once the loop 

completes.  Is there a way to dynamically name and create data objects? 

Additionally I'd like to be able to call those
matrices later for 

example: 

for(i in 1:10){ 

     for(j in 1:10) 

    final.matrix<-sum(object[i] * object[j]) 

}} 

I want object[i] and object[j] to
represent the names of matrices created earlier. Is there a way to create a
reference like this? 

Any suggestions are greatly appreciated

From pburns at pburns.seanet.com  Mon Aug  1 15:46:54 2005
From: pburns at pburns.seanet.com (Patrick Burns)
Date: Mon, 01 Aug 2005 16:46:54 +0100
Subject: Re: creating data objects using loop index
In-Reply-To: <000f01c596a6$38c77360$64fea8c0@Ford>
References: <000f01c596a6$38c77360$64fea8c0@Ford>
Message-ID: <42EE43EE.6070903@pburns.seanet.com>

You want to have a list and use the correct subscripting.

object <- vector('list', 10)
for(i in 1:10) {
   object[[i]] <- A %*% B
}

S Poetry can tell you more.

