From parkhurs at imap.iu.edu  Wed Aug  3 14:53:06 2005
From: parkhurs at imap.iu.edu (David Parkhurst)
Date: Wed, 03 Aug 2005 09:53:06 -0500
Subject: Reference for df in t.test
Message-ID: <42F0DA52.2030107@imap.iu.edu>

I’m using s-plus Enterprise Developer Version 7.0.0  for Microsoft 
Windows : 2005

I am puzzled by the formula given for degrees of freedom in t.test(), 
for the case when var.equal=F.  This is given under “(d) Welch Modified 
Two-Sample t-Test.” in the help page, ?t.test.  That formula differs 
from any other I have seen, and I do not find it in any of the three 
references provided on that help page.  (For example, Snedecor & Cochran 
provide Satterthwaite’s formula, which is different.)  Can someone 
provide a reference for the formula used in t.test?

Thanks.

David Parkhurst

From elinder at cisunix.unh.edu  Wed Aug  3 17:19:10 2005
From: elinder at cisunix.unh.edu (Ernst Linder)
Date: Wed, 03 Aug 2005 13:19:10 -0400
Subject: Re: Reference for df in t.test
References: <42F0DA52.2030107@imap.iu.edu>
Message-ID: <42F0FC8E.60807@cisunix.unh.edu>

Hi Dave:

The equation given in ?t.test is the same as

df = (var.x/nx + var.y/ny)^2 / (var.x^2 / (nx^2*(nx-1)) + var.y^2 / 
(ny^2*(ny-1)))

which is the more standard form as it appears in references such as

De Groot and Schervish: Probability and Statistics, Addison Wesley, 2002
         page 502

or Devore, Prob and Stats for Engineering and the Sciences, 6th ed. 2004
    Thomson Learning.    page 373

Ernst.

