COURSE ANNOUNCEMENT  -  DEPT. OF MATHEMATICS & STATISTICS
FALL 2005:              MATH 969.01   3 credits    CRN #: 11993  :
Topics in Statistics:  Smoothing and Semiparametric Regression

Time and Room:          T R   4:10-6:00 PM MUB DL
Instructor:                   Ernst Linder
2-2687 ,  elinder at unh.edu     4 Mill Road (Kinetic Site),

Topic Description:
Semiparametric regression is concerned with the flexible incorporation
of local non-linear functional relationships in regression analyses.
This is related to penalized smoothing methods for estimating a highly
complex relationship between variables.  Any scientific field that uses
moderate to large amounts of data for knowledge discovery can benefit
from the application of semiparametric regression, in particular data
mining studies that involve continuous response variables.
We will follow a modern approach to nonparametric regression which is
based on combining traditional linear regression with linear methods
applied to expanded bases used for local estimation (smoothing).  From
a statistical point of view we will employ methods from mixed effects
modeling which provide a rich “machinery” of statistical inference tools.
As an added bonus students will learn many current “hot” topics of the
statistical methods toolbox, such as linear and generalized linear mixed
effects models, additive and generalized additive models, spline smoothing,
Bayesian semiparametric regression, and spatially adaptive smoothing.

The goal of the course is to provide a thorough understanding of the
methodologies for semiparametric regression, both in concept and in practice.
Numerous case studies will be discussed from a variety of scientific areas:
pollution studies, econometric analyses, fishery biology, atmospheric studies,
medical and environmental health studies, socio and socio-economic studies.

S-PLUS and/or R software implementation will be discussed and demonstrated.

(Note Equivalent MATLAB code will also be available for those interested).
Distance Learning:      

This course is offered as “FAR-VIEW” synchronously
on-line using LearnLinc:
LearnLinc is a client-server software tool being used as a platform for
Distance Education at the University of New Hampshire. With learnLinc,
students can attend a live class session led by an instructor using their
Windows computer system and an Internet connection. The instructor's voice,
along with course materials, are delivered to both local and off-campus
students in real time.

Learn more about FarView at www.unh.edu/farview

Textbook:  Semiparametric Regression 2003.  David Ruppert, M. P. Wand, R. J. 
Carroll.
   Paperback, Cambridge University Press.  ISBN: 0521785162

Prerequisites:  Intermediate statistics including basics of maximum likelihood
estimation; linear regression modeling incl. familiarity with matrix notation,
basic concepts of calculus incl. partial derivatives.  If you are unsure about
the prerequisites, please contact the instructor.
