Dear list,

 

I am trying to use the Euler discretization functions 'euler.pcode.aux' and 'euler.pcode.gensim' to produce an end-price distribution from simulations of a mean reverting process.

I follow closely the help files and things work fine for a single simulation.

 

### sample listing ###

 

rho.gcir<-c(0.086092008,34.73089057,0.010403578)

 

rho.gcir.names=c("k","mu","sigma")

 

ird.gcir.aux<-euler.pcode.aux(drift.expr=_expression_(k*(mu-X)-0.5*sigma^2),diffuse.expr=_expression_(sigma),rho.names=rho.gcir.names,t.per.sim=1/252,ndt=1,seed=10,X0=34.7)

gcir.sim=euler.pcode.gensim(rho=rho.gcir,n.sim=250,n.var=1,n.burn=100,aux=ird.gcir.aux)

 

######################

 

However, when I try to produce a set of (e.g. 1000) simulations to produce an output matrix (rather than a single vector) things seem to fall apart. I have already experimented with the N and M arguments in the 'euler.pcode.aux' function to increase the dimensionality of the problem but I had no luck.

I have already discovered a sideway to my problem through the use of an lapply with the 'euler.pcode.gensim' function. 

I would have expected though that an NxM simulation could be performed within the body of these functions.

Can anyone else provide some insights to this problem?

Thank you,

 

