Dear all,

I use the following code in combination with Canty's library boot to
calculate mean cost differences of two samples.

dataset.cost <- data.frame(cost.column, data.strata)
diff.means <- function(d, f)
{    n <- nrow(d)
                diff <- 1:table(as.numeric(d$data.strata))[1]
                m1.cost <- sum(d[diff,1] * f[diff])/sum(f[diff])
                m2.cost <- sum(d[-diff,1] * f[-diff])/sum(f[-diff])
                ss1.cost <- sum(d[diff,1]^2 * f[diff]) - (m1.cost *  m1.cost
* sum(f[diff]))
                ss2.cost <- sum(d[-diff,1]^2 * f[-diff]) - (m2.cost *
m2.cost * sum(f[-diff]))
                c(m1.cost-m2.cost, (ss1.cost+ss2.cost)/(sum(f)-2)) 
}
my.boot.cost <- boot(dataset.cost, diff.means, R=R, stype="f",
strata=data.strata)

Now I want to calculate studentized bootstrap intervals with boot.ci
assuming my cost data is lognormally distributed, so I want to execute a log
transformation on my cost data. For this purpose I can use the h and hdot
functions in boot.ci to define my transformations. However, boot.ci uses the
bootstrapped cost differences, but these can be negative. So log
transforming them results in NA's. Is there some other way to correctly log
transform and back transform my cost data with boot.ci and is it possible
with this method to assume that my data is gamma distributed? Your help is
appreciated!

kind regards,

Martijn

