S-plus 2000/winXP

Dear all,

I am using nlme to fit a non-linear growth model with 4 parameters, 2 of
which are supposed to have independent random effects within the same
grouping factor (say gf). 

Additionnaly, one of them (called s) is depending from a categorical
covariate V with 2 levels (1 and 2). After first insight into data and
prefits, it appears that s should be 0 at one of these levels (say 1) and
have a particular value for the other, in which case only a random effect
would be allowed :

So I need  s = s0 + Vi   i = 1,2

with  s0 + V1 = 0  and  s0 + V2 = V2  (implying constraints s0 = 0 and V1 =
0) and a random effect on V2

My idea was to combine :

* the use of "treatment" contrasts with : contrasts (data$V) <-
contr.treatment(2), so that contrasts (data$V) appears as : 
        1  0
        2  1

* the removal of intercept by using the  "-1" expression when specifying
both fixed and random effects with :

        fixed = list (p1 + p2 + p3 ~ 1, s ~ V - 1)
        random = pdDiag (list (p1 ~ 1, s ~ V - 1))
        groups = ~ gf

It appeared that nlme ignored the use of -1 : it required 2 starting values
for V effect estimation to start , and the 2 same estimations were provided
in each case (-1 or not) : an intercept close to 0 and V2.

After reading "Modern applied statistics with S-plus" by Venables and
Ripley, §6.2, it seems that using either contrasts specification or the
"-1" expression in formula are alternative procedures to make parameter
identification possible. Whether they can be combined in some way is not
explicitely mentioned.

Is there someone that could help me ?

With great many thanks.

JDB

