From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Aug 12 07:16:13 2005
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Fri, 12 Aug 2005 08:16:13 +0100
Subject: Re: varcomp() vs lme()
In-Reply-To: <OFB63CC0BD.C9199D0E-ON8725705A.0079BDCC-8725705A.007A9A3B@usgs.gov>
References: <OFB63CC0BD.C9199D0E-ON8725705A.0079BDCC-8725705A.007A9A3B@usgs.gov>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.61.0508120811200.31398@gannet.stats>

On Thu, 11 Aug 2005, Brian S Cade wrote:

> While this is not a question specifically associated with S-Plus, it still
> seems relevant to post here.  In a simple 1-way classification example
> like the rails data in Pinheiro and Bates, Mixed-effects models in S and
> S-Plus, one might estimate the variance components using lme() as in they
> did but wouldn't this also classically be analyzed with varcomp()?  Is
> there a simple summary of why they would differ and which method is
> preferable (seesm like most people are now using lme() for this type of
> analysis).

There is also aov() with an Error term (the really classical analysis). 
If the data are balanced, all three approaches give the same results 
(worked examples in MASS).  If not, they differ, and probably the REML 
approach in lme() is the most satisfactory with varcomp() providing 
alternatives.

lme() is much the slowest: this used to matter a decade ago but probably 
does not any more.

